## Why This Question Is Asked

This problem tests dynamic programming with turn constraints on grid paths, requiring careful state design and path reconstruction. It evaluates understanding of edge case handling and performance optimization for large inputs.

## Key Concepts

- **4D DP state**: position (i, j), turn count (t), and ending direction (d)
- **Two transition types**: continuing in the same direction versus making a turn
- **Path reconstruction**: using parent pointers or backtracking to retrieve the actual path
- **Time and space trade-offs**: consider optimization for large grids

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(M, k) {
  const n = M.length;
  const m = M[0].length;
  const dp = Array(n).fill(null).map(() =>
    Array(m).fill(null).map(() =>
      Array(k + 1).fill(null).map(() => [-1, -1])
    )
  );

  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = M[0][0];

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        for (let d = 0; d < 2; d++) {
          if (dp[i][j][t][d] < 0) continue;

          if (i + 1 < n) {
            const newD = 1;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i + 1][j][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i + 1][j]
              );
            }
          }

          if (j + 1 < m) {
            const newD = 0;
            const newT = d === newD ? t : t + 1;
            if (newT <= k) {
              dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD] = Math.max(
                dp[i][j + 1][newT][newD],
                dp[i][j][t][d] + M[i][j + 1]
              );
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return Math.max(dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][0], dp[n - 1][m - 1][k][1]);
}
```

## Complexity

- **Time**: O(n × m × k)
- **Space**: O(n × m × k)

## Why This Is Asked
This tests deriving a clean DP where subarray cost depends on endpoints, not internal aggregates, and requires careful index handling and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP state dp[i][t] for first i elements into t blocks
- Transition over split point p with endpoint cost |A[p+1]-A[i]| 
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints
- Time O(n^2 k), space O(nk) (can optimize with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minEndPointPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = Math.abs(A[0] - A[i]);
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + Math.abs(A[p + 1] - A[i]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let endpoints = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = par[i][t];
    endpoints.unshift([p + 1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { cost: dp[n - 1][k], endpoints };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if cost were |A[l]-A[r]|^2 or involved a different function?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue tricks if the cost has structure?

## Why This Is Asked
Combinatorial DP with partitioning; tests clear recurrence and reconstruction, common in interviews. 

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t] = min over p of dp[p][t-1] + max(A[p..i-1]);
- Track parent to reconstruct partition points; iterate i and t in increasing order.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = Math.max(...A.slice(0,i));
  for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
    for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for(let p=i-1; p>=t-1; p--){
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[p]);
        const cand = dp[p][t-1] + curMax;
        if(cand < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; par[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {cost: dp[n][k], par};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time using a deque-based technique when max is a sliding property? 
- How would you extend to allow hypothetical negative numbers or to reconstruct explicit subarrays?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle: fixed window size with non-overlap, maximizing sum of window maxima.

## Key Concepts
- Precompute windowMax to make transitions O(1).
- Prefix DP with non-overlapping constraint.
- Reconstruction via a take/parent table.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKWindows(A, n, k, m){
  const windowMax = Array(n+1).fill(-Infinity);
  for(let i=m; i<=n; i++){ // window ending at i
    let mx = -Infinity;
    for(let j=i-m+1; j<=i; j++) mx = Math.max(mx, A[j]);
    windowMax[i] = mx;
  }
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for(let i=0; i<=n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let t=1; t<=k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if(i>=m && dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i] > dp[i][t]){
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-m][t-1] + windowMax[i];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem introduces a dynamic programming angle where subarrays are weighted by their block index. It tests the ability to transform interval sums into prefix sums, derive a two-dimensional DP over position and block count, and implement solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums for O(1) interval sum computation
- 2D DP over position and block count
- Solution reconstruction via parent pointers
- Time complexity: O(n²k); Space complexity: O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScorePartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const P = [0];
  for (let x of A) P.push(P[P.length-1] + x);
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    dp[i][1] = P[i];
  }

  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let p = t-1; p < i; p++) {
        const score = dp[p][t-1] + (P[i] - P[p]) * t;
        if (score > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = score;
          parent[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct partitions
  const partitions = [];
  let end = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = parent[end][t];
    partitions.unshift([start+1, end]);
    end = start;
  }

  return { maxScore: dp[n][k], partitions };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests time-expanded DP on grids for path counts with constraints.

## Key Concepts
- DP on (i,j,t) layers; space optimization; modulo arithmetic.
- Handling walls and boundary checks.

## Code Example
```javascript
function countPaths(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const MOD = 1_000_000_007;
  if (grid[0][0] === 1 || grid[n-1][m-1] === 1) return 0;
  let prev = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(m).fill(0));
  prev[0][0] = 1;
  const dirs = [[1,0],[-1,0],[0,1],[0,-1]];
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    const cur = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(m).fill(0));
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      if (grid[i][j] === 1) continue;
      for (const [dx,dy] of dirs) {
        const x = i + dx, y = j + dy;
        if (x>=0 && x<n && y>=0 && y<m && grid[x][y]===0) {
          cur[i][j] = (cur[i][j] + prev[x][y]) % MOD;
        }
      }
    }
    prev = cur;
  }
  return prev[n-1][m-1];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests nuanced DP on partitioning with range-dependent block costs, requiring problem decomposition, state design, and path reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and block counts
- Efficient range max computation in transitions
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(nums, m) {
  const n = nums.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(m+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= Math.min(i, m); t++) {
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for (let j = i-1; j >= t-1; j--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, nums[j]);
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + curMax * (i - j);
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          par[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct endpoints
  const ends = [];
  let i = n, t = m;
  while (t > 0) {
    ends.push(i);
    i = par[i][t];
    t--;
  }
  return {score: dp[n][m], ends: ends.reverse()};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This variation tests DP with exact count of non-overlapping segments and reconstruction, a common analytics pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Non-overlapping subarray DP
- EndHere vs global best states
- Pointer-based reconstruction
- Memory/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k){
  const n=A.length, INF=-1e18;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-INF));
  const end=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(-INF));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      end[i][t]=Math.max(end[i-1][t]+A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1]+A[i-1]);
      dp[i][t]=Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

